{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_yzfjkf54hcffullcymfujaigku5almwtrgy6chzhbx4sqa3ygeca","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Rust implementation is complete and builds successfully. Here's a summary of what I implemented:\n\n## Summary\n\nTranslated the C minidb (`mdb`) key-value database implementation into idiomatic, safe Rust. All `unimplemented!()` placeholders have been replaced with working implementations:\n\n**Public API:**\n- `Mdb::create` – Creates 3 files (`.db.super`, `.db.index`, `.db.data`), writes superblock as text, initializes index file with a free pointer (0) followed by `hash_buckets` zeroed bucket pointers.\n- `Mdb::open` – Reads the text superblock to reconstruct options and re-opens index/data files for read+write.\n- `Mdb::read` – Hashes key, walks the bucket's linked-list chain via `read_index`, returns data via `read_data`.\n- `Mdb::write` – Inserts new key (with `index_alloc`/`data_alloc`/`write_data`/`write_index`/`write_nextptr` plus rollback on error) or updates existing key (frees old data and writes new).\n- `Mdb::delete` – Walks chain, frees data and index slot, unlinks via `write_nextptr`.\n- `get_options`, `index_size`, `data_size` – Straightforward accessors.\n\n**Internal helpers** mirror the C implementation precisely, including:\n- The free-list allocation strategy for index records\n- The C data allocation algorithm scanning for `>= size + 2` zero bytes (with `+1` offset)\n- The mdb hash function (replicating C signed-char promotion)\n- Little-endian byte serialization for `u32` pointer/size fields\n\nBuild result: `cargo build` finishes successfully with only style warnings on the pre-existing enum variant names (which the rules forbid me from modifying)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":252,"cache_read_input_tokens":54721,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens"